WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.418
Authority and scope come before collection, sharing, or action.

00:00:03.418 --> 00:00:07.842
A team should be able to answer basic questions before it begins:

00:00:07.842 --> 00:00:12.266
who requested the work, what decision it supports, what information is needed,

00:00:12.266 --> 00:00:16.690
which systems or records are in scope, which jurisdictions or policies apply,

00:00:16.690 --> 00:00:20.745
who may receive the results, and who must review sensitive steps.

00:00:20.745 --> 00:00:24.800
If the answers are not known, that is a coordination issue,

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:26.644
not an invitation to improvise.

00:00:26.644 --> 00:00:28.487
Jurisdiction is broader than geography.

00:00:28.487 --> 00:00:32.542
It can include national or regional law, employment rules, contractual limits,

00:00:32.542 --> 00:00:35.860
sector regulations, government requirements, data residency expectations, evidence rules,

00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:37.704
partner agreements, and internal policy.

00:00:37.704 --> 00:00:39.916
A corporate fraud team, a SOC,

00:00:39.916 --> 00:00:43.602
and a public-sector coordination office may all use intelligence processes,

00:00:43.602 --> 00:00:46.551
but their authorities and constraints can differ sharply.

00:00:46.551 --> 00:00:49.501
Even inside one organization, separate subsidiaries, business units,

00:00:49.501 --> 00:00:52.081
or regulated functions may have different rules.

00:00:52.081 --> 00:00:55.768
This course does not provide legal advice or jurisdiction-specific procedures.

00:00:55.768 --> 00:01:00.929
The operational lesson is to recognize boundary conditions and route uncertainty to qualified reviewers.

00:01:00.929 --> 00:01:03.510
Legal, privacy, compliance, records, HR, policy owners,

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:06.459
and governance stakeholders are not obstacles to coordination.

00:01:06.459 --> 00:01:08.671
They are part of lawful coordination.

00:01:08.671 --> 00:01:13.464
They help determine what information may be collected, how it can be retained,

00:01:13.464 --> 00:01:17.150
which identifiers should be minimized, whether cross-border sharing is allowed,

00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:19.731
and whether an action requires additional approval.

00:01:19.731 --> 00:01:22.312
Scope also protects the quality of analysis.

00:01:22.312 --> 00:01:28.210
A defined scope keeps the team focused on the decision rather than chasing every interesting lead.

00:01:28.210 --> 00:01:32.634
It limits unnecessary exposure of personal data, sensitive business information, partner data,

00:01:32.634 --> 00:01:34.109
or restricted source material.

00:01:34.109 --> 00:01:39.270
It makes escalation clearer because everyone understands what the effort is intended to answer.

00:01:39.270 --> 00:01:42.588
When authority is unclear, pause and document the uncertainty.

00:01:42.588 --> 00:01:43.694
Ask for review.

00:01:43.694 --> 00:01:48.855
Record who approved the scope, what limits apply, and what conditions would require reapproval.

00:01:48.855 --> 00:01:53.279
That discipline protects the organization, the analyst, the affected people or entities,

00:01:53.279 --> 00:01:55.860
and the usefulness of the intelligence product.
